WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.660
 

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.180
Before computers and digital cameras,
movies were edited hand.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.960
Editors would get rolls of raw film and
physically cut and paste the scenes together.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.050
This process was called SPLICING. But what does this

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.769
old editing technique have to do with
human disease? "It runs in the family."

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:29.949
Chances are, you've heard this phrase before.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.020
Today, we know that the 'biological
details of our bodies'

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.489
passed down from our parents. This includes physical traits,

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:40.359
like your hair color, or height, but also
your susceptibility to certain kinds

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:41.170
of disease.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.399
All this information encoded

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:47.399
in your DNA. DNA is made up

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:50.550
four nucleotide bases, A, G, C

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.800
and T that spell out the information required for a human cell to carry out the

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.390
various functions of like

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.539
These are organized into units called genes.

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:04.320
Now, every cell in your body contains
workers called ribosomes.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.990
Ribosomes build proteins, and each protein
carries out a specific task in the

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.140
cell.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.310
However, ribosomes need instructions every
time they put together a protein.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.159
inside every cell there is a nucleus, and
inside every nucleus,

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:23.920
there is a copy of your DNA. Think of the 
nucleus as a media center.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.729
It produces tutorials that teach ribosomes
how to build proteins,

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:31.740
and right now, we need to build a protein
to strengthen the walls in the nucleus.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.000
We'll call it Protein X.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.090
Imagine your DNA is like a strip of film.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.280
From here to hear is one gene.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.439
Inside this gene is the whole tutorial
for building protein X.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:48.899
how do we make the tutorial? First,

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:50.620
the nucleus copies this gene.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.560
This copy is called pre-mRNA, and can be
edited.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.720
The original DNA is

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.430
preserved for future reference. But the
tutorial is not edited yet,

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.860
and the footage is separate.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.940
Part of it is here, another part is here,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.070
another part is here. These frames with
footage

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.640
are called exons. The film strip

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.010
also contains frames that aren't parted of the 
video.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:25.360
The sequences are called introns and
will not be in the final tutorial.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.940
One of their purposes is to provide
instructions on how

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.040
and where to edit the film. This

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.800
is the spliceosome.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.090
Think pf it as a film editor. It  follows
directions in

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.590
introns, cuts them out, and paste the
exon's together.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.500
This entire process is known splicing.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.560
Now we have a coherent tutorial video that 
can be relayed to the ribosomes.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.670
Splicing is crucial to the correct
construction of proteins.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.650
Unfortunately, mistakes can occur

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:02.050
as a result of mutations. Mutations are
variations from the nucleotide sequences

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.530
in our original DNA. We used to think

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.180
only mutations in the movie frames
themselves caused disease.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.500
But now, we know that mutations that
affect the splicing process

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.690
can also cause disease! Back to protein X.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.940
If there are no mutations in the film,
Protein X will look like this,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:26.300
and fit perfectly into the cell wall. But
let's say there is a mutation in the film

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:26.940
frames.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.100
This might cause Protein X to have a
little hole in it, like this.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.270
When it slots in, it leaves a gap in the
wall,

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.850
and you get a slightly weaker structure.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.820
Now, let's say there's a splicing mutation: a mutation in instructional frames.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.210
This will cause the organization, rather than the content of the video to be altered.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.250
some scenes might be missing, others might 
be cut short,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.160
and others may include unnecessary segments of film.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.080
This will have a much bigger effect on
how Protein X is built.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.720
The finished protein might have an entire
region missing, like this.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.670
And this is happening in
many cells at once,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:10.400
so imagine the kinds of damage that spicing mutations can do.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.270
so why is spicing important? Experts
estimate that one third of

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.350
all heritable diseases involves  an error in 
spicing.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.180
Still, we don't have a detailed
understanding of how exactly spicing

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.920
goes wrong.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:28.570
Part of the problem is that each person
has about 3 million unique

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.789
differences in their DNA, so it's hard to
tell which ones cause disease.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:36.689
Today, researchers are using computers

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:39.889
and statistics to comb through mountains
of mutations

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:44.759
and model their effect on splicing. We
hope to find patterns that tell us how

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:47.059
splicing is directed.

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:51.339
Eventually, doctors may be able to come
up with personalized treatments,

00:04:51.339 --> 00:04:55.299
by studying the mutations that affect how our
genetic tutorials

00:04:55.299 --> 00:04:59.369
are edited

00:04:59.369 --> 00:05:47.629
 

